@Jamie Sigadel @Laura Lopez I know we planned to have this in our widget (and we probably should still), but it feels odd in the Firefox Settings to "cherrypick" one thing that gets a direct link to Support, when technically anything on these pages can be a reason for a user to write in with questions.

I think we should remove it from here.

cc' @Maysarah Shahabuddin

@Jamie Sigadel

Is this how lists are going to be managed in the "manage exceptions" section above ?

cc' @Maysarah Shahabuddin

@Jamie Sigadel @Laura Lopez 

Above there is an example of website-level exceptions (see first block). Should we embrace that wording? (e.g. "Manage Exceptions", followed by an explanatory text).

cc' @Maysarah Shahabuddin

@Jamie Sigadel @Laura Lopez

Dunno if placement was discussed, but it feels to me that Firefox VPN should go above "Additional protections". Wdyt?

cc' @Maysarah Shahabuddin

@Laura Lopez - A few Qs - 

1. What kind of preferences would be set here? Would it list anything MORE than whether a website has protection ON or OFF? 

2. What will be listed here? All websites that ARE protected? Or, all websites that AREN'T protected? (I assumed the latter - otherwise it would need to list every website everywhere :upside_down_face:)

@Santiago Andrigo @Jamie Sigadel Hi both! I see there's lot of comments around my explorations here on my information architecture (IA) and happy to walk you through these when I return. 

At a high-level, I want to flag that these are concepts for how we might organize the information. If we are treating each variant as an isolated experiment (vs. comparing them apples to apples) it may make sense to group different pieces of information together to support comprehension. 

Anywho! I will follow up with both of you together to do a walkthrough of each. Thanks!

@Jamie Sigadel In Delta and Gamma, users can exclude a website from protection directly in the VPN panel, whereas here they have to click through.

Why not offer them a way to opt-out here, like you do in the other versions?

(the other consequence of changing feature discoverability is that we may bias the comparisons between versions)

@Jamie Sigadel

I think that if the user has used up their bandwidth, we can probably just remove all controls and replace them with a stronger message about upgrading, where we afford an illustration, bullet points, etc.

We should also include a mention that their bandwidth will be restored at the beginning of the month (assuming we use calendar months, which is being discussed elsewhere).

@Wolasi Konu FYI that for iOS (and macOS) we won't be able to offer neither the ability to exclude a website NOR to set a different geolocation. This is due to OS limitations.

So, to the extent that you may create an iOS prototype, those features wouldn't be there.

cc' @Jamie Sigadel

@Jamie Sigadel @Sally Richardson @Jennifer Palais 

Oh! hadn't thought of this. Love it. Maybe we display some form of checklist for users who've self identified as privacy and security oriented?

I think this concept probably falls within "continuous onboarding" in some way. The onboarding team might have some thoughts on this or might want to adopt this UX pattern (for other user profiles) if they haven't thought about it.

